<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muscat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muscat is a port city on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, commanding sea routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a> conquered it in about 1743, extending Iranian commercial and naval reach. The conquest reflected Afsharid maritime ambitions that later Iranian dynasties proved unable to sustain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muscat.jpg" alt="Muscat" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tristan · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_Alam_Palace.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Persian Gulf trade through Bushehr and Bandar Abbas continued, and Nader's intervention in Oman, culminating in the capture of <b>Muscat</b> around 1743, extended Iranian reach into the Arabian Sea, but these revenues were insufficient to sustain an empire held together by military force.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>When Said died at sea in 1856, his sons divided his realm: Thuwaini took <b>Muscat</b> and Oman, while Majid bin Said became sultan of Zanzibar and the African dominions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1913 Ibadi scholars and tribal leaders elected Salim ibn Rashid al-Kharusi as imam at Tanuf; his forces took Nizwa and threatened <b>Muscat</b>, which was saved by British Indian troops.</blockquote><a href="/state/imamate-of-oman">Imamate of Oman</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings controlled ports on both sides of the Gulf, including Qalhat, <b>Muscat</b> and Bahrain, and paid tribute to stronger mainland powers.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ormus">Kingdom of Ormus</a></li><li><blockquote>The port of Gwadar on its coast belonged to <b>Muscat</b> until 1958.</blockquote><a href="/state/makran-state">Makran State</a></li><li><blockquote>His family, the Al Bu Said, took the title of sayyid and later sultan and made <b>Muscat</b> their capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/omani-empire">Omani Empire</a></li></ul></section>
